<sn><md>### Basic Command Structure

```bash
rclone COMMAND SOURCE_REMOTE:PATH DESTINATION_REMOTE:PATH
```

For example:

```bash
rclone sync gdrive:Documents mega:Documents
```

Here:

```text
gdrive:Documents ───────────► mega:Documents
☁️ Google Drive                 ☁️ MEGA

SOURCE                          DESTINATION
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Using `copy`

```text
BEFORE

☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE                  ☁️ MEGA

📁 Documents                     📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt                     ├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt                     └── 📄 Old.txt
└── 📄 C.txt


COMMAND

rclone copy gdrive:Documents mega:Backup


AFTER

☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE                  ☁️ MEGA

📁 Documents                     📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt                     ├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt ─────────────────► ├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt ─────────────────► ├── 📄 C.txt
                                 └── 📄 Old.txt
```

✔️ Source cloud files remain.
✔️ Extra files in the destination remain.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Using `move`

```text
BEFORE

☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE                  ☁️ MEGA

📁 Documents                     📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt


COMMAND

rclone move gdrive:Documents mega:Backup


TRANSFER

☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE  ─────────────►  ☁️ MEGA
      A.txt                           A.txt
      B.txt                           B.txt
      C.txt                           C.txt


AFTER SUCCESSFUL TRANSFER

☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE                  ☁️ MEGA

📁 Documents                     📁 Backup
                                 ├── 📄 A.txt
                                 ├── 📄 B.txt
                                 └── 📄 C.txt
```

✔️ Files are transferred to the destination cloud.
❌ Source files are removed after a successful transfer.
✔️ Extra destination files remain.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Using `sync`

Suppose the source cloud contains:

```text
☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE

📁 Documents
├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt
```

But the destination cloud contains:

```text
☁️ MEGA

📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 Old.txt
```

Run:

```bash
rclone sync gdrive:Documents mega:Backup
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Synchronization Process

```text
☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE                  ☁️ MEGA

📁 Documents                     📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt ─────────────────► 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt ─────────────────► 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt ─────────────────► 📄 C.txt
                                 ❌ Old.txt
```

**After Synchronization**

```text
☁️ GOOGLE DRIVE                  ☁️ MEGA

📁 Documents                     📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt                     ├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt                     ├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt                     └── 📄 C.txt
```

The destination cloud becomes identical to the source cloud.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Summary

**Example: Google Drive to MEGA**

```bash
# Copy files
rclone copy gdrive:Documents mega:Backup

# Move files
rclone move gdrive:Documents mega:Backup

# Synchronize files
rclone sync gdrive:Documents mega:Backup
```

**Important Rule**

```text
rclone sync SOURCE DESTINATION

SOURCE  ─────────────►  DESTINATION
         controls
        everything
```

⚠️ `sync` can delete files from the **destination**. Always test an important cloud-to-cloud synchronization first:

```bash
rclone sync gdrive:Documents mega:Backup --dry-run
```

This allows you to see the planned changes without modifying either cloud storage.
</md></sn>